23 │  * than a question about who the caller is.
24 │  *
25 │  * <p><b>An {@link AuthorizationManager} rather than a filter of our own.</b> The rule then
26 │  * sits on the chain beside the permits it qualifies, where the whole policy can be read in
27 │  * one method, and it runs at the point in the chain that already knows how to translate a
28 │  * refusal into a response. A filter would be a second place authorization happens.
29 │  *
30 │  * <p><b>It grants nothing, and it authenticates nobody.</b> There is no principal here and
31 │  * nothing downstream learns who called: presenting the secret means a request is allowed to
32 │  * proceed, not that this application knows which Check service made it. Telling those apart
33 │  * is what {@code docs/deferred.md} calls for under a real service identity, and this class is
34 │  * deliberately not a step towards one.
